17592186045417] [17592186314318 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186312663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314318 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186312864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314318 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186313099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314318 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314318 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314318 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186312284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314318 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7167d3ef-020b-5c26-98b0-e6af7ebfa802" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314337 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186311959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314337 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186312039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314337 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186312347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314337 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186312758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314337 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186313897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314337 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314337 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314337 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186313897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314337 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7b0d7e63-bf87-56e8-ad2d-ee39fe6ec062" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314815 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314815 #edb/ref 1069 "q-339664" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314815 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314815 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/52/528bf1bc9d6177f031b8b5504641903a11dfc2c2576e4759bf4cb7c278d0585c.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $y=0$ is a sink\n\nII. $y=3$ is a source\n\nIII. $y=3$ is a node" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314815 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b869e45a-6b1f-53e1-aacc-10ffe2baaefd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314815 #edb/ref 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=0$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points upward. In other words, both arrows point *toward* the point $y=0.$ This means that $y=0$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=3$ points upward, and the arrow just below it points downward. In other words, both arrows point *away* from $y=3.$ This means that $y=3$ is a source.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315094 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315094 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34142" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315094 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315094 #edb/ref 1071 "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315094 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5d50385e-e4f4-5265-bb61-4639aa5b495f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315094 #edb/ref 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]=-3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]=-3{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}-3{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=3$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315321 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315321 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315321 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "24069f64-c580-5200-b5e0-1c7ef2ae7e68" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315451 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315451 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315451 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3a15928d-e29a-514a-aa9c-b81e928ccc22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315724 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315724 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315724 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6b92d802-3085-528a-9c72-efc57cf5c2c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315821 #edb/ref 1080 "None of the statements are true" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315821 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315821 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "800f5135-a705-5bc5-b182-cd0428d00e1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315941 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315941 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315941 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "93c3185c-8c40-5110-addf-07d04037df9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316627 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186315321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316627 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186315451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316627 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186315724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316627 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186315821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316627 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186315941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316627 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316627 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316627 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186315451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316627 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "22bb0e27-d6d9-5966-9fb5-317681ddbc7c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317320 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317320 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34276" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317320 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317320 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n\nIII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317320 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1120ac93-1146-5212-b323-41cae9ac4014" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317320 #edb/ref 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements I and II are false, while statement III is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317752 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317752 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317752 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "39f8e453-5b4e-5424-8df3-89ef36cde644" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317756 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{5}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317756 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317756 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3b9175b7-617f-57a3-af8d-919ed2b0db92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318048 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{3}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318048 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318048 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8e503817-bf60-566b-9a9f-488b451dfc84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318271 #edb/r